in/grocery/cart/service/CartLimitExceededException.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.cart.service;

import java.net.URI;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;
import org.springframework.web.ErrorResponseException;

public class CartLimitExceededException extends ErrorResponseException {

    public CartLimitExceededException(int max) {
        super(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, problem(max), null);
    }

    private static ProblemDetail problem(int max) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY,
                "A cart can hold at most " + max + " different items");
        pd.setType(URI.create("https://errors.grocery.in/cart-item-limit"));
        pd.setTitle("Cart item limit exceeded");
        pd.setProperty("code", "CART_ITEM_LIMIT_EXCEEDED");
        pd.setProperty("maxItems", max);
        return pd;
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>No handler needed — Spring recognizes <code>ErrorResponse</code> exceptions.</p> <p>Coupling</p> <p><code>ErrorResponseException</code> ties a service-layer exception to HTTP. Fine for small services; in the four-layer platform we keep domain exceptions HTTP-free and map them in one place (Level 4).</p>

in/grocery/platform/error/ValidationProblemHandler.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.platform.error;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import org.springframework.context.support.DefaultMessageSourceResolvable;
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatusCode;
import org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.validation.FieldError;
import org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;
import org.springframework.web.context.request.WebRequest;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.ResponseEntityExceptionHandler;

@RestControllerAdvice
class ValidationProblemHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {

    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity&lt;Object&gt; handleMethodArgumentNotValid(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex,
                                                                  HttpHeaders headers,
                                                                  HttpStatusCode status,
                                                                  WebRequest request) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ex.getBody();                                                  // (1)!
        pd.setTitle("Validation failed");
        pd.setDetail("One or more fields are invalid");
        pd.setProperty("code", "VALIDATION_FAILED");
        List&lt;Map&lt;String, Object&gt;&gt; errors = ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
                .map(fe -&gt; Map.&lt;String, Object&gt;of(
                        "field", fe.getField(),                                          // (2)!
                        "code", fe.getCode() == null ? "Invalid" : fe.getCode(),
                        "message", messageFor(fe)))
                .toList();
        pd.setProperty("errors", errors);
        return handleExceptionInternal(ex, pd, headers, status, request);
    }

    private String messageFor(FieldError fe) {
        return getMessageSource() != null
                ? getMessageSource().getMessage(fe, org.springframework.context.i18n.LocaleContextHolder.getLocale())  // (3)!
                : fe.getDefaultMessage();
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Start from Spring's problem so <code>status</code>/<code>instance</code> stay consistent.</li> <li><code>address.pincode</code>, <code>items[2].quantity</code> — maps directly onto the client form.</li> <li>Localized via <code>Accept-Language</code> (Chapter 09's bundles).</li> </ol> Result<pre><code>{
  "type": "about:blank",
  "title": "Validation failed",
  "status": 400,
  "detail": "One or more fields are invalid",
  "instance": "/api/checkout",
  "code": "VALIDATION_FAILED",
  "errors": [
    { "field": "address.pincode", "code": "Pincode", "message": "Enter a valid 6-digit pincode" },
    { "field": "items[0].quantity", "code": "Max", "message": "must be at most 20" }
  ]
}
</code></pre> <p>One <code>ResponseEntityExceptionHandler</code> per application</p> <p>If you extend it, disable Boot's own (<code>spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=false</code> or leave it on — Boot backs off when an advice extending <code>ResponseEntityExceptionHandler</code> exists). Two advices both handling the same framework exceptions leads to order-dependent behaviour.</p>

Additions to the handler<pre><code>@Override
protected ResponseEntity&lt;Object&gt; handleHandlerMethodValidationException(
        HandlerMethodValidationException ex, HttpHeaders headers, HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ex.getBody();
    pd.setTitle("Validation failed");
    pd.setProperty("code", "VALIDATION_FAILED");
    List&lt;Map&lt;String, Object&gt;&gt; errors = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    ex.getParameterValidationResults().forEach(result -&gt;                                  // (1)!
            result.getResolvableErrors().forEach(err -&gt; errors.add(Map.of(
                    "field", result.getMethodParameter().getParameterName(),
                    "message", getMessageSource() != null
                            ? getMessageSource().getMessage(err, LocaleContextHolder.getLocale())
                            : String.valueOf(err.getDefaultMessage())))));
    pd.setProperty("errors", errors);
    return handleExceptionInternal(ex, pd, headers, status, request);
}

@ExceptionHandler(ConstraintViolationException.class)                                    // (2)!
ProblemDetail constraintViolation(ConstraintViolationException ex) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "One or more values are invalid");
    pd.setTitle("Validation failed");
    pd.setProperty("code", "VALIDATION_FAILED");
    pd.setProperty("errors", ex.getConstraintViolations().stream()
            .map(v -&gt; Map.of("field", leafPath(v.getPropertyPath().toString()), "message", v.getMessage()))
            .toList());
    return pd;
}

private static String leafPath(String path) {          // "reserve.reservations[0].quantity" → "reservations[0].quantity"
    int dot = path.indexOf('.');
    return dot &lt; 0 ? path : path.substring(dot + 1);
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>One result per failing parameter; each has resolvable errors (message codes). <code>getParameterName()</code> needs <code>-parameters</code>.</li> <li>From <code>@Validated</code> services (Chapter 09). Mapped to 400 here because in this platform services called from web requests validate client data; for internal-only callers, a violation is a bug — you may prefer 500 there.</li> </ol> <p>Verify</p> <p>Accessor names on <code>HandlerMethodValidationException</code> (<code>getParameterValidationResults</code>, <code>getAllValidationResults</code>, visitor API) evolved between Framework 6.1, 6.2 and 7.0. Check the Javadoc for your version.</p>

