in/grocery/platform/client/PooledHttpClientConfig.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.platform.client;

import java.time.Duration;
import org.apache.hc.client5.http.config.ConnectionConfig;
import org.apache.hc.client5.http.config.RequestConfig;
import org.apache.hc.client5.http.impl.classic.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.hc.client5.http.impl.classic.HttpClients;
import org.apache.hc.client5.http.impl.io.PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager;
import org.apache.hc.client5.http.impl.io.PoolingHttpClientConnectionManagerBuilder;
import org.apache.hc.core5.util.TimeValue;
import org.apache.hc.core5.util.Timeout;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.client.ClientHttpRequestFactory;
import org.springframework.http.client.HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class PooledHttpClientConfig {

    @Bean
    ClientHttpRequestFactory pooledRequestFactory(HttpClientProperties props) {
        PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager pool = PoolingHttpClientConnectionManagerBuilder.create()
                .setMaxConnTotal(props.maxTotal())                    // e.g. 100
                .setMaxConnPerRoute(props.maxPerRoute())              // e.g. 50 — the real limit per dependency
                .setDefaultConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.custom()
                        .setConnectTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(1))
                        .setSocketTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(2))
                        .setValidateAfterInactivity(TimeValue.ofSeconds(5))    // (1)!
                        .setTimeToLive(TimeValue.ofMinutes(5))                 // (2)!
                        .build())
                .build();

        CloseableHttpClient client = HttpClients.custom()
                .setConnectionManager(pool)
                .evictIdleConnections(TimeValue.ofMinutes(1))
                .setDefaultRequestConfig(RequestConfig.custom()
                        .setConnectionRequestTimeout(Timeout.ofMillis(200))    // (3)!
                        .setResponseTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(2))
                        .build())
                .disableAutomaticRetries()                                     // (4)!
                .build();

        return new HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory(client);
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Revalidate connections idle longer than 5 s — avoids "connection reset" from silently dropped sockets.</li> <li>Retire connections before load balancers or NATs do (keep below their idle timeout).</li> <li>Fail fast when the pool is exhausted instead of queuing — the caller gets a clear dependency error and the circuit breaker can open.</li> <li>Retries are a policy decision made one level up (5.5), not a silent client default.</li> </ol> <p>Expose pool metrics (Chapter 27) so "pending connection leases" is visible before it becomes latency.</p>

in/grocery/cart/client/PricingRetryConfig.java<pre><code>@Bean
PricingApi pricingApi(RestClient pricingRestClient, RetryTemplate pricingRetryTemplate) {
    PricingApi delegate = HttpServiceProxyFactory
            .builderFor(RestClientAdapter.create(pricingRestClient)).build()
            .createClient(PricingApi.class);
    return new RetryingPricingApi(delegate, pricingRetryTemplate);                          // (1)!
}

@Bean
RetryTemplate pricingRetryTemplate() {
    return RetryTemplate.builder()
            .maxAttempts(3)
            .exponentialBackoff(50, 2.0, 400, true)                                          // (2)!
            .retryOn(DependencyException.class)
            .withListener(new MetricsRetryListener("pricing-service"))
            .build();
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>A decorator retries only the idempotent methods (<code>price</code>, <code>prices</code>) — a <code>POST /bulk</code> that only reads is idempotent in practice; document that assumption with the dependency's owner.</li> <li>50 ms, 100 ms, 200 ms with jitter, capped — total worst case ~350 ms of backoff plus 3 × 800 ms of timeouts. That must still fit the caller's budget: with a 2 s SLA, use 2 attempts and a 600 ms timeout.</li> </ol> <p>Retry storms</p> <p>Retrying at every layer multiplies load: gateway (3) × order-service (3) × pricing client (3) = 27 requests for one user action, exactly when the dependency is already struggling. Retry at one layer only, and pair retries with a circuit breaker (Chapter 26).</p>

