in/grocery/catalog/service/CategoryService.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.catalog.service;

import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.CacheEvict;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class CategoryService {

    private final CategoryRepository categories;

    public CategoryService(CategoryRepository categories) {
        this.categories = categories;
    }

    @Cacheable(cacheNames = "categories", key = "'active'")                       // (1)!
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List&lt;CategoryView&gt; activeCategories() {
        return categories.findByActiveTrueOrderByNameAsc().stream().map(CategoryView::from).toList();
    }

    @CacheEvict(cacheNames = "categories", allEntries = true)                     // (2)!
    @Transactional
    public void rename(String slug, String newName) {
        categories.findBySlug(slug).orElseThrow().rename(newName);
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>A single-entry cache needs an explicit constant key (a no-arg method would otherwise use <code>SimpleKey.EMPTY</code>, which works but is less readable).</li> <li>Category lists are small and change rarely — evicting everything is simplest and safe here.</li> </ol> <p>Evict after commit</p> <p><code>@CacheEvict</code> runs when the method returns — which, inside a transaction, is before commit. Another thread can repopulate the cache from pre-commit state. For write paths, either evict after commit (Chapter 16's synchronization or a <code>@TransactionalEventListener</code>), or accept the short window for tolerant data.</p>

