in/grocery/search/service/ProductSearchService.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.search.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ProductSearchService {

    public record SearchRequest(String query, String pincode, List&lt;String&gt; brands,
                                java.math.BigDecimal maxPrice, String cursor, int size) {}
    public record SearchHit(String sku, String title, String brand, String price, double score) {}
    public record Facet(String name, Map&lt;String, Long&gt; counts) {}
    public record SearchResponse(List&lt;SearchHit&gt; hits, long total, List&lt;Facet&gt; facets, String nextCursor) {}

    private final SearchClient client;         // thin wrapper over the OpenSearch/Elasticsearch Java client

    public SearchResponse search(SearchRequest request) {
        var query = SearchQuery.builder()
                .must(multiMatch(request.query(), Map.of(                                    // (1)!
                        "title", 3.0f,
                        "brand", 2.0f,
                        "tags", 1.0f))
                        .fuzziness("AUTO")                                                   // (2)!
                        .minimumShouldMatch("2&lt;75%"))
                .filter(term("inStockPincodes", request.pincode()))                          // (3)!
                .filter(request.brands().isEmpty() ? null : terms("brand", request.brands()))
                .filter(request.maxPrice() == null ? null : range("sellingPrice").lte(request.maxPrice()))
                .functionScore(fieldValueFactor("popularity", 0.3f))                         // (4)!
                .aggregation(terms("brand").size(20))
                .aggregation(range("sellingPrice", 0, 100, 250, 500, 1000))
                .sort("_score", "desc").sort("sku", "asc")                                   // (5)!
                .searchAfter(decodeCursor(request.cursor()))
                .size(Math.min(request.size(), 50))
                .build();
        return client.search("products", query);
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Field boosts: a match in the title matters more than in tags.</li> <li>Typo tolerance — "aata", "atat" still find atta. Fuzziness costs CPU; keep it <code>AUTO</code> (edit distance scaled by term length).</li> <li>Filters, not queries, for structured constraints: cached, no scoring cost. Stock is modelled as a list of serviceable pincodes on the document — denormalized for search, authoritative elsewhere.</li> <li>Popularity boost blended with text relevance.</li> <li>A tiebreaker field is required for <code>search_after</code> to be stable.</li> </ol> <p>Client libraries — verify</p> <p>Spring Data Elasticsearch targets the official Elasticsearch client; OpenSearch has its own community <code>spring-data-opensearch</code> project and the <code>opensearch-java</code> client. Pick per your cluster and confirm version compatibility before writing a lot of code; the query structures above are the same either way.</p>

in/grocery/search/indexer/ProductIndexer.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.search.indexer;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ProductIndexer {

    private final SearchClient search;
    private final ProductProjectionLoader loader;
    private final MeterRegistry meters;

    @KafkaListener(topics = {"grocery.catalog.product-changed.v1", "grocery.inventory.stock-changed.v1"},
                   groupId = "search-indexer", batch = "true")                                 // (1)!
    public void onEvents(List&lt;CatalogEvent&gt; events) {
        var docs = loader.loadProjections(events.stream().map(CatalogEvent::sku).distinct().toList());  // (2)!
        search.bulkIndex("products", docs);                                                     // (3)!
        meters.counter("grocery.search.indexed").increment(docs.size());
        events.stream().map(CatalogEvent::occurredAt).max(java.util.Comparator.naturalOrder())
              .ifPresent(t -&gt; meters.gauge("grocery.search.index.lag.seconds",
                      Duration.between(t, java.time.Instant.now()).toSeconds()));               // (4)!
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Batch listener (Chapter 23): indexing 500 documents in one bulk request instead of 500 round trips.</li> <li>Events carry ids; the projection is loaded from PostgreSQL so the index is always built from the source of truth — events are a trigger, not the data.</li> <li>Bulk API with retry on partial failures (OpenSearch returns per-item status).</li> <li>Index lag is the metric that matters: "how stale can search be right now?"</li> </ol>

in/grocery/search/admin/ReindexService.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.search.admin;

import java.time.Instant;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ReindexService {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ReindexService.class);

    private final SearchClient search;
    private final ProductProjectionLoader loader;

    public String reindex(String mappingVersion) {
        String target = "products_" + mappingVersion + "_" + Instant.now().getEpochSecond();
        search.createIndex(target, Mappings.load(mappingVersion));                              // (1)!

        long indexed = loader.streamAll(batch -&gt; search.bulkIndex(target, batch), 1_000);       // (2)!
        log.info("Bulk indexed {} products into {}", indexed, target);

        long live = search.count("products");
        if (indexed &lt; live * 0.99) {                                                            // (3)!
            throw new IllegalStateException("Refusing to swap: new index has %d docs vs %d live".formatted(indexed, live));
        }
        SmokeQueries.run(search, target);                                                       // (4)!

        search.swapAlias("products", target);                                                   // (5)!
        log.info("Alias 'products' now points at {}", target);
        return target;
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>New index with the new mapping — analyzers and field types can change freely.</li> <li>Streamed from PostgreSQL in batches (Chapter 12's streaming), so memory stays flat for 2M products.</li> <li>Guard rail: never swap to an index that's obviously incomplete.</li> <li>Relevance smoke tests: "atta" returns Aashirvaad in the top 3, "dahi" returns curd, an out-of-stock pincode filters correctly. Automate the queries a human would check.</li> <li>Atomic alias swap: the next query goes to the new index; the old one stays for instant rollback.</li> </ol> <p>Events that arrive during the bulk load are handled by keeping the indexer running against both indices (dual write) or by replaying the Kafka topic from the offset recorded at the start of the reindex.</p>

