in/grocery/order/service/OrderCancellationService.java<pre><code>@Service
public class OrderCancellationService {

    private final JdbcClient jdbc;
    private final OrderRepository orders;
    private final MeterRegistry meters;

    /** Cancels in bounded chunks; safe to run every minute from any pod. */
    public int cancelUnpaidOlderThan(Duration age) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int batch = 0; batch &lt; 10; batch++) {                                     // (1)!
            List&lt;String&gt; refs = jdbc.sql("""
                    select order_ref from customer_order
                    where status = 'PLACED' and placed_at &lt; :cutoff
                    order by placed_at
                    limit 200
                    for update skip locked
                    """)                                                               // (2)!
                    .param("cutoff", Instant.now().minus(age))
                    .query(String.class).list();
            if (refs.isEmpty()) break;
            refs.forEach(this::cancelOne);                                             // (3)!
            total += refs.size();
        }
        meters.counter("grocery.orders.auto-cancelled").increment(total);
        return total;
    }

    @Transactional
    void cancelOne(String orderRef) {
        orders.findByOrderRef(orderRef).ifPresent(order -&gt; {
            order.cancel();
            outbox.append("order", orderRef, "OrderCancelled", new OrderCancelled(orderRef, "unpaid-timeout"));
        });
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Bounded work per run: a backlog after downtime is drained over several runs instead of one hour-long transaction.</li> <li><code>SKIP LOCKED</code> means multiple pods could even run this concurrently without conflict — ShedLock becomes an optimization, not a correctness requirement.</li> <li>One short transaction per order (Chapter 16), so a failure on order 37 doesn't roll back the first 36.</li> </ol>

