in/grocery/payment/gateway/RazorpayResilientGateway.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.payment.gateway;

import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class RazorpayResilientGateway {

    private final RazorpayApi api;

    @Retry(name = "razorpay-read")                                            // (1)!
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "razorpay")
    @Bulkhead(name = "razorpay")
    public PaymentStatus status(String paymentId) {
        return api.payment(paymentId).toStatus();
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "razorpay")                                        // (2)!
    @Bulkhead(name = "razorpay")
    public String createOrder(String orderRef, BigDecimal amount) {
        return api.createOrder(new RazorpayApi.OrderRequest(
                amount.movePointRight(2).longValueExact(), "INR", orderRef, java.util.Map.of())).id();
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "razorpay")                                        // (3)!
    public RefundResult refund(String paymentId, long amountPaise) {
        return api.refund(paymentId, new RazorpayApi.RefundRequest(amountPaise, java.util.Map.of())).toResult();
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Status lookups are idempotent reads — safe to retry.</li> <li>Order creation is not retried automatically here even though Razorpay dedupes by receipt (Chapter 19): the breaker and bulkhead protect us, and the platform's idempotency aspect (Chapter 04) handles client retries. One retry layer.</li> <li>Refunds are never retried automatically; a failed refund becomes a reconciliation task.</li> </ol> <p>Per-operation breakers vs per-dependency: use one breaker per dependency when failures are usually systemic (the whole service is down), and separate breakers per operation when one endpoint can fail independently (e.g. a heavy report endpoint on an otherwise healthy service).</p>

in/grocery/platform/resilience/LoadShedFilter.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.platform.resilience;

import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.filter.OncePerRequestFilter;

/** Priority-aware concurrency limiting: protects checkout when the service is saturated. */
public class LoadShedFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private final Semaphore total;
    private final Semaphore lowPriority;                                      // (1)!
    private final io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry meters;

    public LoadShedFilter(int maxConcurrent, int maxLowPriority, io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry meters) {
        this.total = new Semaphore(maxConcurrent);
        this.lowPriority = new Semaphore(maxLowPriority);
        this.meters = meters;
    }

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        boolean low = isLowPriority(req);
        Semaphore gate = low ? lowPriority : total;

        if (!gate.tryAcquire()) {                                              // (2)!
            meters.counter("grocery.load.shed", "priority", low ? "low" : "high").increment();
            res.setStatus(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE.value());
            res.setHeader("Retry-After", "2");
            res.setContentType("application/problem+json");
            res.getWriter().write(problemJson("OVERLOADED", 503, "Service is busy, please retry"));
            return;
        }
        try {
            chain.doFilter(req, res);
        } finally {
            gate.release();
        }
    }

    private static boolean isLowPriority(HttpServletRequest req) {
        String uri = req.getRequestURI();
        return uri.startsWith("/api/v1/search") || uri.startsWith("/api/v1/recommendations");   // (3)!
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Two gates: browsing/search can be shed while checkout and webhooks continue.</li> <li>Reject immediately — a queued request that times out anyway wasted resources twice.</li> <li>The priority list is a product decision, reviewed like any other business rule.</li> </ol> <p>Where load shedding belongs</p> <p>The gateway (Chapter 35) is the better place for global limits and per-client quotas; a service-level filter protects against internal callers and gateway misconfiguration. Doing it in both is fine — doing it in neither is how overload becomes an outage.</p>

