in/grocery/order/domain/Money.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.order.domain;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.Currency;

public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {                      // (1)!

    private static final Currency INR = Currency.getInstance("INR");

    public Money {
        if (amount.scale() &gt; 2) {
            amount = amount.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);                    // (2)!
        }
        if (amount.signum() &lt; 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Money cannot be negative: " + amount);
        }
    }

    public static Money rupees(String amount) {
        return new Money(new BigDecimal(amount), INR);
    }

    public Money plus(Money other) {
        requireSameCurrency(other);
        return new Money(amount.add(other.amount), currency);                     // (3)!
    }

    public Money times(int quantity) {
        return new Money(amount.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(quantity)), currency);
    }

    public boolean isGreaterThan(Money other) {
        requireSameCurrency(other);
        return amount.compareTo(other.amount) &gt; 0;                                // (4)!
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>A record: immutable, equality by value — exactly what a value object needs.</li> <li>Invariants enforced in the compact constructor; an invalid <code>Money</code> cannot exist.</li> <li>Operations return new instances.</li> <li><code>compareTo</code>, not <code>equals</code> — <code>BigDecimal.equals</code> treats <code>1.0</code> and <code>1.00</code> as different (Chapter 11).</li> </ol> in/grocery/order/domain/Pincode.java<pre><code>public record Pincode(String value) {
    private static final Pattern VALID = Pattern.compile("[1-9][0-9]{5}");

    public Pincode {
        if (value == null || !VALID.matcher(value).matches()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid Indian pincode: " + value);   // (1)!
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Validation once, at construction. Every method receiving a <code>Pincode</code> knows it's valid — no defensive checks scattered through the codebase (contrast with passing <code>String</code> everywhere, Chapter 09).</li> </ol>

in/grocery/order/domain/Order.java<pre><code>package in.grocery.order.domain;

import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Order {                                                              // (1)!

    private static final int MAX_LINES = 50;

    private final OrderRef ref;
    private final CustomerId customerId;
    private final Pincode pincode;
    private final List&lt;OrderLine&gt; lines = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    private OrderStatus status;
    private Money deliveryFee;
    private Instant placedAt;
    private final List&lt;DomainEvent&gt; events = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();                    // (2)!

    private Order(OrderRef ref, CustomerId customerId, Pincode pincode) { ... }

    public static Order place(OrderRef ref, CustomerId customerId, Pincode pincode,
                              List&lt;OrderLine&gt; lines, Money deliveryFee) {
        if (lines.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyOrderException(ref);                                    // (3)!
        }
        if (lines.size() &gt; MAX_LINES) {
            throw new TooManyLinesException(lines.size(), MAX_LINES);
        }
        Order order = new Order(ref, customerId, pincode);
        lines.forEach(order.lines::add);
        order.deliveryFee = deliveryFee;
        order.status = OrderStatus.PLACED;
        order.placedAt = Instant.now();
        order.events.add(new OrderPlaced(ref, customerId, order.total()));         // (4)!
        return order;
    }

    public void markPaid(PaymentId paymentId) {
        if (status != OrderStatus.PLACED) {
            throw new IllegalOrderTransitionException(ref, status, OrderStatus.PAID);   // (5)!
        }
        status = OrderStatus.PAID;
        events.add(new OrderPaid(ref, paymentId, total()));
    }

    public void cancel(String reason) {
        if (!status.isCancellable()) {
            throw new IllegalOrderTransitionException(ref, status, OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
        }
        status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
        events.add(new OrderCancelled(ref, reason));
    }

    public Money subtotal() {
        return lines.stream().map(OrderLine::lineTotal)
                .reduce(Money.rupees("0.00"), Money::plus);                        // (6)!
    }

    public Money total() {
        return subtotal().plus(deliveryFee);
    }

    public List&lt;OrderLine&gt; lines() {
        return Collections.unmodifiableList(lines);                                // (7)!
    }

    public List&lt;DomainEvent&gt; pullEvents() {
        List&lt;DomainEvent&gt; pulled = List.copyOf(events);
        events.clear();
        return pulled;
    }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>No Spring, no JPA annotations, no framework imports — this class is pure business rules and can be unit-tested in microseconds.</li> <li>Events are collected in the aggregate and published by the application service after save.</li> <li>Invariants are enforced at construction — you cannot build an invalid order.</li> <li>Placing an order is an event; the aggregate records it.</li> <li>State transitions are guarded by the domain, not by a service remembering to check.</li> <li>Totals are derived, never stored inconsistently.</li> <li>Callers can read lines but cannot mutate them behind the root's back.</li> </ol>

